# Virtualenv and caches -- rebuildable from uv.lock.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Archived IBKR statements. DELIBERATELY NOT VERSIONED.
#
# These are raw Activity Statements and they contain the account number, the
# account holder's legal name, every position, every fill and every cash
# balance for the period. Committing them writes that into git history
# permanently: adding the directory later is one command, removing it from
# history is not, and history rewriting is off the table.
#
# The tradeoff is real and worth knowing. The test suite uses these as its
# oracle -- 10 tests are parametrized per archived statement and several more
# skip without them -- so a fresh clone has a weaker suite until `optjournal
# fetch` repopulates this directory. Statements also cost requests against
# IBKR's lockout budget, so treat raw/ as data to back up, not to regenerate
# casually.
#
# To version them anyway:  git add -f raw/
#
# BOTH SPELLINGS, and the second one is not redundant. `raw/` matches a
# directory and does NOT match a SYMLINK named `raw` -- so a link pointing at
# the real archive (the obvious way to lend a worktree the statements the suite
# needs) showed up as untracked and was one `git add -A` away from committing a
# pointer into this data. Caught twice by hand during one session, which is
# twice too many for something this file exists to make impossible.
raw/
raw

# Fetch-state sidecar: local request-budget bookkeeping, not shared state.
.fetch-state.json

# Stored preferences: one person's Flex query id and scoreboard unit. A clone is
# someone else's journal, and `optjournal setup` writes this in seconds -- see
# settings.py. The `.tmp` siblings are the atomic-write staging files, which only
# exist mid-write but would be committable if a write were interrupted, and the
# `.lock` is the empty sidecar a save locks (see locks.py).
.optjournal.json
.optjournal*.tmp
.optjournal.lock

# Derived database. Rebuildable at any time with `optjournal ingest`, and a
# WAL-mode SQLite file is a poor fit for git regardless -- binary, no useful
# diff, and two volatile sidecars.
journal.db
journal.db-shm
journal.db-wal
# Timestamped copies of that database, which the three rules above do NOT match:
# a pattern with no slash matches a NAME at any depth, so `journal.db` catches
# `snapshots/journal.db` and misses `snapshots/journal-20260813T085234Z.db`. Each
# snapshot is a whole journal -- one carried the account number, 264 equity
# summaries and 179 trades -- and 7.5MB of them sat here untracked and unignored,
# one `git add -A` from the mistake this file exists to prevent. Two rules for the
# same reason raw/ has two: the directory, and the name, so a snapshot written
# anywhere else is still covered.
snapshots/
journal-*.db
# Cross-process lock files. Empty, per-database, and recreated on demand --
# see locks.py. Committing one would be committing a runtime artifact.
*.migrate.lock
# The fetch lock is the same kind of thing, and it lands in whichever directory
# is serving as an archive -- including tests/fixtures/statements/, which IS
# tracked, so a test run would otherwise leave a file to commit.
.fetch.lock

# Synthetic data from `optjournal demo`. Regenerated deterministically, and
# kept out of raw/ so a fake statement can never be mistaken for a real one.
demo/

# Build symlink and any scratch that lands in it. Ignored because it is not
# source: a probe script and a 471KB scratch database were once swept in by a
# broad `git add -A`, and the archive is the only data this repo is supposed to
# carry.
build/

# Private research notes on the reference implementation: its schema, its
# endpoints, and a cross-check of this account's figures against it. Kept out of
# the repo DELIBERATELY -- it describes someone else's application and quotes real
# account numbers, and neither belongs in a repo meant to be handed to a friend.
#
# Ignored rather than merely left untracked, because untracked is not a defence: a
# broad `git add -A` committed it once, in a commit about something else entirely,
# and the fix was rewriting four commits. An ignore rule makes that impossible
# instead of unlikely -- the same reasoning as the `build/` entry above.
IAG_REFERENCE.md

# Assistant tooling: workflow scripts and session state, not project source.
# Written here by the harness rather than by anyone working on the journal.
.claude/
.kiro/

# Playwright MCP scratch (console logs from browser verification runs).
.playwright-mcp/

# Screenshots from browser verification. The tool writes them to whatever the
# working directory is, which is the repo root, so they land beside the source
# and look like project files. Four (728KB) were swept into a commit about a
# database race and lived on mainline until someone read the file list.
#
# Ignored by EXTENSION rather than by name: the names are invented per run
# ("audit-trades", "chart-negative", "focus-ring"), so a denylist of names
# would always be one run behind. This repo ships no raster images -- the one
# image it does ship is static/mark.svg, and SVG is deliberately not matched
# here -- so anything matching these is scratch by construction.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp

# The rotating application log and launchd's stdout capture. Runtime artefacts of
# a supervised `serve`, like journal.db and raw/ -- the plist that points at them
# IS tracked, under launchd/.
logs/
